<div class="main-title">Likutay Halachos &mdash; Choshen Mishpat II</div>
<div class="sub-title">Hilchos Pikadon v&rsquo;Arba&rsquo;ah Shomrim<br>[Laws of Deposits and the Four Guardians]</div>

<div class="halacha-title">Halacha 1</div>

<p>All the guardians must take an oath. For even a borrower [sho&rsquo;el], in a case where he is exempt &mdash; that is, where it died due to the labor for which it was borrowed &mdash; requires an oath from the Torah. And the guardians require an oath even without a claim against them.</p>

<p>For the matter of the <span class="key-term">shevu&rsquo;ah</span> [oath] is based upon what Rabbainu, may his light shine, wrote in siman 7: that through emes [truth], one comes to emunah [faith], in the aspect of: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Tzedek, when it joins with emes, becomes emunah,&rdquo;</span> as it is taught in the Zohar &mdash; see there.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of the shevu&rsquo;ah. For the speech of Israel is the aspect of <span class="key-term">dibbur hakodesh</span> [the holy speech], the aspect of: <span class="key-term">&ldquo;Malchus &mdash; peh&rdquo;</span> [kingship &mdash; the mouth]. And certainly we do not suspect a Jew of speaking falsehood, G&ndash;d forbid. But when there is a denial &mdash; that his fellow contradicts him &mdash; then it is the aspect of the hold of the Sitra Achra, which is the falsehood, as our Sages of blessed memory said, within the holy speech, which is the speech of truth. And then the speech, which is the aspect of Malchus, as above, is in the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzedek</span>. For tzedek is the holy Malchus, and it is called tzedek when they [the forces of impurity] take hold in it, G&ndash;d forbid, as is brought. And all of this is because the emes was blemished and the falsehood took hold in it, G&ndash;d forbid. And because of this it is called tzedek, as above.</p>

<p>And therefore one must repair the speech, which is in the aspect of: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Tzedek you shall speak&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Tehillim 58:2)</span>, so that it should be the aspect of emunah. And then one will be trusted [ne&rsquo;eman] in his words. And this is accomplished through connecting with emes, in the aspect of: &ldquo;Tzedek, when it joins with emes,&rdquo; etc., as above. And this is the aspect of the shevu&rsquo;ah. For the shevu&rsquo;ah is the aspect of <span class="key-term">hiskashrus</span> [connection/binding], as is brought in Sha&rsquo;arai Orah &mdash; that the shevu&rsquo;ah is in Yesod. And this is the aspect of: <span class="verse">&ldquo;And Yosef made his brothers swear&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Beraishis 50:25)</span> &mdash; that is, the shevu&rsquo;ah is the aspect of connecting the speech to the emes.</p>

<p>And therefore the standard oath is while holding a sacred object [chaifetz], and the custom now is with a Sefer Torah. For the Torah is the aspect of emes, as it is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;A Torah of truth&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Malachi 2:6)</span>, etc. And it is the aspect of Yaakov, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Give emes to Yaakov&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Michah 7:20)</span>. For Yaakov is the aspect of the Torah, as is brought. And therefore one takes an oath while holding a sacred object, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">&ldquo;He who swore to Hashem, who vowed to the Mighty One of Yaakov&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Tehillim 132:2)</span> &mdash; that is, one connects and joins the speech to the aspect of emes, the aspect of Yaakov, in the aspect of: &ldquo;Tzedek, when it joins with emes,&rdquo; and then it &ldquo;becomes emunah,&rdquo; as above. For this is the aspect of the completeness of emunah, as is known. And then one is trusted in his words, for the speech that was in the aspect of tzedek has been repaired and completed through the shevu&rsquo;ah, which is the aspect of hiskashrus, as above.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">shevu&rsquo;ah of the guardians</span>, who must take an oath even without a claim and denial. For there are four guardians, and they are in the aspect of Malchus, which is the aspect of the number four, as is brought. For they guard against theft and loss, so that the money of Israel should not be damaged or harmed &mdash; this is the aspect of the tikkun of the Malchus. For the money is the aspect of the neshamah, as is explained above many times. And therefore most of the rulers and kings of Israel were shepherds beforehand, for the guarding against loss is the aspect of the tikkun of the Malchus, as is written above in Hilchos Avaidah. And therefore there are four guardians, who repair the aspect of Malchus, which is the aspect of the number four. For the king is also the aspect of a guardian, who must oversee the people and guard them so that they should not be scattered, as above.</p>

<p>And this is what is written in the Zohar: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Shamor [&lsquo;guard&rsquo;] &mdash; in the feminine,&rdquo;</span> which is the aspect of Malchus, as above. And therefore the guardians must be very careful in their guarding, as is written in the Shulchan Aruch. And our Sages of blessed memory were very stringent about this, for extra guarding is needed so that the aspect of Malchus should not be blemished &mdash; since it is entrusted to them, as is known.</p>

<p>And therefore, when there is damage &mdash; that it was stolen, or lost, or taken by force &mdash; then it is the aspect of a blemish in the Malchus, as above, and then it is in the aspect of tzedek, as above. And therefore they all need an oath even without a denial. And they are not trusted without an oath, since the aspect of the Malchus was blemished in their case &mdash; in the aspect of tzedek &mdash; through the pikadon [deposit] being lost, as above. And through the shevu&rsquo;ah, which is the aspect of the tikkun of the Malchus, as above &mdash; for initially, through the loss, the Malchus was blemished, and the blemish is that a separation was caused, G&ndash;d forbid, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">&ldquo;The tzaddik has been lost&rdquo;</span> &mdash; and it is taught in the Zohar: &ldquo;What does &lsquo;lost&rsquo; mean?&rdquo; etc. And therefore, through the shevu&rsquo;ah, which is the aspect of hiskashrus, the aspect of Yosef HaTzaddik, as above &mdash; through this, it is repaired and becomes the aspect of emunah, as above. And then specifically the guardians are trusted, as above.</p>

<p>But in all other cases, an oath is not needed except when there is a denial, as above &mdash; which is the aspect of the blemish, as above. But when there is no denial and no blemish, there is certainly no need for an oath.</p>

<p>And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: that one who finds a lost object does not take an oath. And similarly, when no one makes a claim against him and he admits of his own accord, he is one who returns a lost object and does not need to take an oath. For when one returns a lost object &mdash; which is the aspect of the tikkun of the Malchus, returning the souls to their place, as above in Hilchos Avaidah &mdash; therefore he certainly does not need an oath, as above.</p>

<p>But the guardians need an oath without a claim, for the loss of the pikadon is itself the blemish, as above. And all the guardians are exempt when the owner is with him [shemirah biv&rsquo;alim]. For the essential guarding &mdash; that the guardian needs to be extra careful, as above &mdash; is only because the object is distant from its owner. And then damage is common, for when the object is distant from the owner, it is the aspect of the neshamah becoming distant from the kavod [honor], as above in Hilchos Avaidah. And then the neshamah is not in its completeness, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;a weary soul&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Mishlai 25:25)</span>. And when there is no completeness, then they [the forces of impurity] have a hold, G&ndash;d forbid, as is known. And therefore extra guarding is needed when the matter is distanced from the owner and is handed over to a guardian.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of: <span class="verse">&ldquo;If a man gives to his fellow silver or vessels to guard, and it is stolen from the man&rsquo;s house&rdquo;</span> <span class="inline-source">(Shemos 22:6)</span>. For immediately when one gives it to a guardian, damages are common &mdash; thefts and losses, as above. And this is why our Sages of blessed memory were stringent about the guarding of the guardians. And as it is written regarding Yaakov: <span class="verse">&ldquo;By day the heat consumed me,&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="inline-source">(Beraishis 31:40)</span>. Even though we see that the homeowner himself is not so careful in guarding his own possessions &mdash; for when the matter is not with the owner and has been handed to another to guard, then extra guarding is needed from them, as above.</p>

<p>And therefore Moshe Rabbainu, peace be upon him, did not want Israel to be led through a guardian, but only through Him, blessed be He, Himself, as it is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Behold, I am sending an angel before you to guard you on the way,&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="inline-source">(Shemos 23:20)</span>. And Moshe did not want this, and said: <span class="verse">&ldquo;If Your face does not go,&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="inline-source">(Shemos 33:15)</span>, as above.</p>

<p>And therefore shemirah biv&rsquo;alim [guarding while the owner is present] is exempt. For since its owner is with him in his labor, and they too are borrowed to him, etc. &mdash; therefore he is exempt. For the matter is not lost through having been handed over to his care, since its owner is also with him, and the neshamah has not become distant, in the aspect of the distancing of the neshamah from the kavod, as above.</p>

<p class="end-marker">[End of Pikadon v&rsquo;Arba&rsquo;ah Shomrim, Halacha 1]</p>

